00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.820
Leah: Let’s have a look
at Scrum in more detail

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:08.270
Chris: Scrum is an Agile methodology
often called a framework to help

00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:10.200
teams to work together on a project.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:15.560
Scrum is quick and easy to adapt, it
has continuous learning cycles, it

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:21.100
enables fast feedback loops, has clear
visibility and low risk, protects the

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:26.190
team from un-prioritised work requests,
and is focused on delivering value.

00:00:27.309 --> 00:00:30.779
Leah: The sprint is a fixed length
event which has a sprint goal.

00:00:31.349 --> 00:00:35.579
All events are time-boxed so once
a sprint begins, it is fixed.

00:00:36.119 --> 00:00:40.000
The sprint itself, involves a collection
of ‘tasks’ for the team to achieve.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.760
Each task or ‘user story’ is
allocated a level of complexity

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:46.320
called a ‘story point’.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:51.510
A sprint project board which is also
known as the ‘Sprint Backlog’ is used

00:00:51.519 --> 00:00:56.580
to track the tasks, and sub-tasks
are positioned in three columns - ‘To

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.680
do’, ‘In progress’, and ‘Done’.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:02.550
Chris: The Scrum Team [also known
as the Scrum Development Team]

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:05.160
organises and manages its own work.

00:01:05.719 --> 00:01:09.190
They deliver the agreed product
increment inside a sprint.

00:01:09.549 --> 00:01:13.280
The team consists of different skillsets
and abilities needed to deliver the

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:17.670
product which would likely include
programming, designing, testing,

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:21.570
content writing, and/or business
skills depending on the project.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.049
Leah: Sprint planning is the
first meeting of the sprint.

00:01:25.049 --> 00:01:30.039
It is where achievable items or user
stories, are identified from the product

00:01:30.039 --> 00:01:32.360
backlog to be moved to the next sprint.

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:36.660
The team would decide in this meeting,
what can be done within the next

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:41.060
sprint, and then refine the user
stories; for example, they would

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:45.720
determine story-points, how tasks
will get done, the definition of

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.559
done, dependencies, and sub-tasks.

00:01:49.539 --> 00:01:54.399
Chris: The Scrum Master manages the
Scrum Process and remove impediments.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.130
They ensure that the framework is
followed and would predominately

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.930
manage the planning meeting taking
into consideration priority, capacity,

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.420
and the wider business expectations.

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:10.350
However, before the planning meeting
can take place, the Product Owner

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:14.580
would need to determine the priority
tasks and decide on the sprint goal.

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:19.379
The Product Owner manages the
Product Backlog and optimises

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:20.779
the value of the Product.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:25.239
Team members also need to allocate their
own tasks on the product ‘backlog’.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:30.890
Leah: The Daily Scrum, also known as
the ‘Daily Standup’, is a 15-minute

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:35.100
meeting for the internal scrum team,
which is arranged at the same time,

00:02:35.139 --> 00:02:36.550
each working day of the sprint.

00:02:37.139 --> 00:02:41.509
Its main objective is to determine
progress and update the project board,

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:46.600
also known as the ‘sprint backlog’, and
adapt the ‘product backlog’ if needed.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:52.730
The focus is on progress and actions, and
self-management is encouraged, as well

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:57.790
as promoting collaboration, improvement,
and transparency amongst team members.

00:02:58.039 --> 00:03:01.530
Chris: The Sprint Review is where the
scrum team present their results from the

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:06.210
work they have done in the current sprint,
to the stakeholders and/or the customers.

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:10.119
[The Product Owner would have direct
relationships with the Business

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:14.329
Owner and the Stakeholders.] It
is where feedback is given, and

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:18.460
adaptations, alterations, changes and
or improvements can be planned for.

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:23.670
The sprint review is a working session,
not just a presentation and the

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:27.959
product backlog is adjusted to reflect
new information and or feedback.

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:31.359
It is usually one of the
last events of the sprint.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:37.120
Leah: The Sprint Retrospective or retro is
the last meeting and concludes the sprint.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:41.480
This is where the team determine what went
well, what could be improved, what needs

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:45.700
greater understanding, and the actions
that need to be taken, moving forward.

00:03:46.349 --> 00:03:49.750
The retro is a safe place where
the team can come together, give

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:51.790
support and productive feedback.

00:03:52.039 --> 00:03:56.109
The purpose of the retro is to act on
what has been reviewed, and plan ways

00:03:56.110 --> 00:03:58.400
to increase quality and effectiveness.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:02.140
One of the biggest benefits of
working in Agile is ‘continuous

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:06.130
improvement’, and in Scrum the driver
of improvement is the ‘Retrospective’.

